        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Installation & Operation</h3>
            <p>CAUTION: Disconnect power supply cord from power source before changing the blade or making any other repair or adjustment to the saw.</p>
            <ol style="font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.5;">
                <li>Visually inspect machine for hidden shipping damage.</li>
                <li>As part of the receiving inspection, check for broken ball bearings on the bearing carrier assembly. This is the assembly shown on page 4.</li>
                <li>IMPORTANT Model 3000 and Model 4000 owners note: The shut off rail was removed for shipping. You must mount the rail on the machine table before using the saw.</li>
                <li>Pull hydraulic pin at bottom of hydraulic. Head is now free to raise to vertical position. Raise head up and over center. Hold head and gently let it move up to vertical position. DON'T LET THE HEAD DROP! See illustration on page 8.</li>
                <li><span style="font-weight: bold;">Vertical Saw Positioning</span></li>
                <li>2. Slide the vertical saw table into the blade and against the back of the horizontal table. Secure with the screwless vise or C-clamps. Adjust the drive end of the blade guide assembly, if necessary, to provide support for that end of the vertical saw.</li>
                <li>3. Attach the vertical blade guard on the moveable guide arm and secure with wing nut supplied.</li>
                <li>4. Position moveable arm as close to work as possible.</li>
                <li><span style="font-weight: bold;">Removing and Replacing Blade</span></li>
                <li>9. By turning the T-handle, apply tension to the blade until it is snug. Slide the blade off the saw wheel.</li>
                <li>10. Slide the blade off the saw wheel.</li>
                <li>11. Remove the blade guide assembly.</li>
                <li>12. Place the new blade over the idler and drive wheels with the guide. The teeth should point out toward you and the tips of the teeth should point toward the motor end of the saw.</li>
                <li>A fast check is to compare the blade (as you place it over the idler wheel) with the decal on the top of the saw head. Check to make sure the blade is on both orange wheels.</li>
                <li>A video is available showing the proper procedure for removing and replacing blades, saw adjustments, troubleshooting and maintenance. If you did not receive a video with this machine or would like another copy, call the factory.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div class="center" style="font-size: 24px; margin-bottom: 30px;">Installation & Operation</div>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Troubleshooting Crooked Cuts</h3>
            <ol style="font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.5;">
                <li>Check to make sure that the number of teeth per inch on the blade is correct for the material being cut.</li>
                <li>Check to make sure that the blade is in contact with the workpiece.</li>
                <li>Check the head pressure on the saw. The compensating spring tension should be 8 pounds with the blade 1" above the table, coming down with the hydraulic valve open.</li>
                <li>Check the blade tension. Review the proper blade tension procedure under "Removing and Replacing Blade" on page 5, item 10.</li>
                <li>Check the space between bearings of both guide bearing assemblies. It should be only .001" over the thickness of the blade.</li>
            </ol>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Saw Adjustments</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 12px;">Measurements should be made with the tools kept as close as possible to the guide bodies. If the blade is not square with the table, the guide bearing plates need to be adjusted to bring the blade into alignment. See illustration of Blade Guide Assembly on page 2.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Blade Speeds</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 12px;">For MEDIUM speed, the middle pulley is used on both shafts. For LOW speed, the smallest pulley on the motor shaft is used with the largest on the reducer shaft.</p>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Replacement of Hydraulic Oil</h3>
            <ol style="font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.5;">
                <li>Remove pipe plug from near top of cylinder.</li>
                <li>Fill with light weight hydraulic oil until oil flows back out of hole. Replace pipe plug.</li>
            </ol>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Maintenance</h3>
            <ol style="font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.5;">
                <li>Check oil level in hydraulic. Proper level is at pipe plug hole.</li>
                <li>Oil in wormgear should be checked every 90 days. FILL TO PROPER LEVEL AT PLUG, BELOW THE BREATHER VENT, USING 80 to 140 weight gear oil.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

